<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">Developer (Forge CLI)</data>
      <data key="d6">UserCompanyDeveloper</data>
      <data key="d7">Developer writes code in JavaScript or TypeScript and deploys it with the Forge CLI. To the developer it looks like a normal JavaScript function, which Forge translates into a Lambda function.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">EC2 Deployment Web Service</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Web service that receives code deployments from developers. Sits behind an ALB so it can scale to handle load from thousands of customers. Stores deployment state in DynamoDB and queues deployment requests onto SQS instead of creating Lambdas directly.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">DynamoDB Deployment State</data>
      <data key="d6">DynamoDB</data>
      <data key="d7">Stores the state of every deployment (in progress, completed, failed, etc.) so the outcome can be reported back to the user.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">SQS Deployment Queue</data>
      <data key="d6">SQS</data>
      <data key="d7">Queues deployment requests because the Lambda control plane limits how many Lambdas can be created in a given time interval. Decouples ingestion from Lambda creation to avoid rate limiting.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">EC2 Deployment Worker</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Back-end service that reads deployment requests from SQS and creates the Lambda in its own time, when it is allowed to, so it is not rate limited.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">Lambda Customer Code</data>
      <data key="d6">Lambda</data>
      <data key="d7">Runs customer code in a separate AWS account that contains nothing else (account boundary from the Forge platform account). Multi-tenant: a Lambda can be shared between multiple customers. On every request a V8 isolate (JavaScript V8 engine construct, like a tiny VM with nothing in it) is spun up to run the customer's code and deleted afterwards, so global variables and state are not shared across invocations. No data is stored in the Lambda.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6">
      <data key="d5">Browser User</data>
      <data key="d6">UserConsumerWeb</data>
      <data key="d7">Browser that invokes the deployed app and renders the page fragment that is returned.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="7">
      <data key="d5">EC2 Invocation Service</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Internal service that checks security permissions (is the user behind the browser allowed to execute that Lambda), finds the right Lambda associated with that user and product, and invokes it with the correct parameters.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Developer deploys JavaScript/TypeScript code through the CLI to the web service.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Stores deployment state to track progress (completed, failed, etc.).</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Queues the deployment request onto SQS instead of creating the Lambda directly, to avoid Lambda control-plane rate limits.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="0" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Reports back to the user what happened to the deployment.</data>
      <data key="d10">6</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Web service reads the deployment state/progress.</data>
      <data key="d10">5</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Back-end service reads (polls) deployment requests from the queue.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Creates the Lambda function with the customer code when allowed to; the Lambda is in a different account (crosses the account boundary).</data>
      <data key="d10">4</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Lambda returns data; a page fragment is constructed and returned to the browser, which renders it.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Browser calls the internal service to invoke the app.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="7" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">After the permission check and Lambda lookup, invokes the right Lambda and sends it the correct parameters, since no data is stored in the Lambda.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">Atlassian Forge: Multi-Tenant Serverless Platform for Custom App Deployment and Invocation</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H2fOkeXxpyw</data>
    <data key="d2">Atlassian Forge lets customers, developers and third-party vendors build custom apps that extend Atlassian products. It has two parts: deployment of customer code (CLI -&gt; EC2 web service -&gt; DynamoDB/SQS -&gt; EC2 worker -&gt; Lambda) and invocation of that code (browser -&gt; EC2 internal service -&gt; Lambda -&gt; browser). The Forge platform and the customer-code Lambdas run in separate AWS accounts (account boundary). Lambdas are multi-tenant; a V8 isolate is created per request and deleted afterwards to isolate tenants. SQS decouples deployment requests to avoid Lambda control-plane rate limiting. Serves thousands of customers.</data>
    <data key="d3">Serverless, SaaS, Multi-Tenant, Security, Developer Platform</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
